course: dictionary
lesson: 11153
title: The small word will
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Will is one of the most useful words in English. Most of the time it tells you what is going to happen. «It will rain» means rain is coming. Will is a helper word: it sits in front of another verb, like help in «Will you help me?»
explain: Would is its close cousin. It talks about what might happen, or what someone imagines. «I would fly if I could» is not a real plan; it is a wish about something pretend. Look for clues like if, could or might to spot would.
explain: Will can also make a promise. «I will be there» means you are saying you truly plan to come. Short forms help too: I'll means I will, you'll means you will, and won't means will not. Quick talk may use «gonna» instead, but in writing and tests, use will.
explain: Will is also a noun. It can mean a strong wish, as in «She has the will to win». A will is also a paper that says who gets someone's things after they pass away. Context tells you which meaning you need: is will a helper before a verb, or a thing you can hold?
fact: Will is a modal verb: it helps another verb, as in «It will rain».
fact: «Would» talks about what might happen or what is imagined: «I would fly if I could».
fact: «Won't» is short for will not, and «I'll» is short for I will.
fact: A will (noun) can mean a strong wish, or a paper saying who gets someone's things after they pass away.
fact: In rules and exams, you may see «shall» or «would be» used in a formal way, such as «you will be required to show your working».
exercise: In «One chocolate bar will not cause a breakout», which short form means the same as will not? | gonna | I'll | you'll | *won't
exercise: «It will rain tomorrow.» What job does will do in this sentence? | It means very wet | It is a paper that says who gets things | It is a strong wish | *It tells us what is going to happen
exercise: Mira says, «I would fly if I could.» Why is would used? | *Because she is imagining something that is not real | Because it is raining now | Because she is making a firm promise | Because she has a will to win
test: In «I'll think about it», what does I'll mean? | *I will | I would | I have | I am
test: «Grandma's will says her garden goes to her son.» What does will mean here? | *A paper saying who gets someone's things after they pass away | A kind promise | Something that is going to happen soon | A strong wish to win
exam: In «I will be there», what does will show? | *A promise that you plan to come | A rule about cricket | Something that happened yesterday | A list of things left behind
